  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe stalled frontal boundary and associated waves of low pressure \ncontinue to trend further south. Scattered to broken clouds are \ndeveloping this afternoon in the somewhat moist easterly flow. It \nwill remain on the cooler side, with a steady east wind 5 to 10 mph \nand mostly cloudy conditions the rest of today. There will be a bit \nmore in the way of sunshine through thin high clouds for the \nSyracuse metro and Mohawk Valley region. High temperatures range in \nthe 60s this afternoon.\n\nThe clouds will tend to dissipate and slowly drop south heading into \nthe overnight period. This will open the door for some cooler \novernight lows, and some very patchy fog for the deeper river and \ncreek valleys of Central NY. Overnight lows will mainly be in the \n50s, with some localized mid to upper 30s possible across portions \nof northern Oneida County and the coldest sheltered valleys of the \nupper Susquehanna region in Central NY. While some very patchy frost \ncan't be ruled out in northern Oneida County, coverage was not \nenough to issue an advisory at this time.\n\nWednesday features more sunshine, as drier air continues to advect \nin from the northeast under the expansive 1040mb surface high \npressure system. After the cool start, it will warm up nicely into \nthe 60s under mostly sunny skies for the afternoon hours. Steady \neast winds between 5 to 15 mph are expected during the day. Wednesday \nnight could be the coolest night of the week, under clear skies and \nrising pressures. Lows looks to range from the upper 30s to mid-\n40s, with some localized mid-30s and patchy frost possible.\n\nThursday and Friday see high pressure remaining in control as the \nsurface high slides west toward the upper Great Lakes. This will \nkeep a dry northeast to northerly flow over our local area. Expect \nplenty of sunshine each day, along with mild temperatures in the 60s \nto near 70 for highs. Overnight lows will be in the upper 30s to mid-\n40s once again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nFrom Friday night through the end of the weekend it appears a slow \nmoving coastal low pressure system will take shape off the northern \nMid-Atlantic or southern New England coast. The latest model \nguidance has come into better agreement that our area will remain \ndry Friday night and likely most of Saturday. As the low spins off \nthe New England coast Saturday night and Sunday, it will run into a \ndeveloping Omega block over the Great Lakes region. Some moisture \nfrom the coastal low could wrap back around into eastern portions of \nour forecast area. For now the ensemble approach continues to show a \nslight chance to low end chance for showers late Saturday through \nSunday. There is a lot of dry air in the mid levels and subsidence \nunder the developing 500mb ridge/block near or just west of our area \nthough. We will continue to monitor trends in the model guidance \nover the coming days, but overall the impacts locally from this \ncoastal low appear to be minimal to perhaps none. From I-81 west \n(and perhaps the entire area) it is becoming very likely that it \nremains dry over the next 7 to 10 days. Temperatures are expected to \ngradually warm up heading into early next week, with highs in the \nlow to mid-70s and overnight lows in the mid 40s to lower 50s expected.",
